### Compare useEffect execution behavior across dependency configurations

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/learn/synchronizing-with-effects.md

Illustrates how omitting the dependency array, passing an empty array `[]`, and passing dependency values `[a, b]` alter when the effect runs.

```js
useEffect(() => {
  // This runs after every render
});

useEffect(() => {
  // This runs only on mount (when the component appears)
}, []);

useEffect(() => {
  // This runs on mount *and also* if either a or b have changed since the last render
}, [a, b]);
```

--------------------------------

### Effect dependencies are determined by the reactive values the effect code reads

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/reference/react/useEffect.md

This section of the useEffect reference states that you cannot 'choose' the dependencies — every reactive value used by the Effect's code must be declared as a dependency, so the dependency list is determined by which reactive values the effect reads. A plain side-effect call like a non-hook push that doesn't read a reactive value is not part of dependency determination, so removing such a call doesn't change the dependency array unless that call was itself reading a reactive value.

```javascript
function ChatRoom({ roomId }) { // This is a reactive value
  const [serverUrl, setServerUrl] = useState('https://localhost:1234'); // This is a reactive value too

  useEffect(() => {
    const connection = createConnection(serverUrl, roomId); // This Effect reads these reactive values
    connection.connect();
    return () => connection.disconnect();
  }, [serverUrl, roomId]); // ✅ So you must specify them as dependencies of your Effect
  // ...
}
```

